  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThis plan employs a multi-resolution strategy with adaptive coordinate descent and local optimization to minimize the maximum overlap integral. It builds upon the parent solution by analyzing the structure of the overlap and introducing directional optimization. The core idea is to start from the parent solution and repeatedly double the resolution, using critical point analysis of the correlation function to guide both greedy coordinate descent and local optimizations. The rationale is that refining the resolution allows detection of correlations that might be hidden at lower resolutions, while directional optimization systematically reduces high-overlap regions.\nThe limitations include not using global optimization methods due to time constraints, and not automatically adjusting hyperparameters. It's an iterative improvement approach that depends on the parent solution's quality.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe represent h as a vector of length n (n even) with values between 0 and 1, and \u2211h_i = n/2 (from the integral constraint). The objective is:\nC5 = max_k dx * correlation of h with (1-h) at lag k\nwhere dx=2.0/n\n\nWe use three mechanisms:\n1. Critical point analysis: Finds the lag k_max with highest correlation and focuses optimization at \u00b1k_max\n2. Projected gradient steps: Reduces overlap for specific points by moving them away from one-hot or pure-zero positions\n3. Local nonlinear optimization: Uses SLSQP with integral constraint and bounds\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Validate parent solution (if provided)\n2. Start with parent solution (n=256) or generate base candidate\n3. Maintain active_lag (the best lag found for coordinate descent) and current_best\n4. For each doubling of resolution (n_new = n*2):\n   a. Interpolate parent to new n\n   b. Find optimal lag (k_max) via correlation scan\n   c. Perform:\n      - Adaptive coordinate descent: Move points away from \u00b11/0 based on lag\n      - Local SLSQP optimization with lag constraint focus\n      - Alternating random search\n   d. Project to feasible solution if needed\n5. Evaluate lowest C5 found\n\nSearch choices:\n- Resolution doubling: Test all multiples of 256 up to 4096 if time permits\n- Lag identification: Use full correlation scan then focus on \u00b13 lags\n- Optimization schedules: Start with L-BFGS-B then SLSQP with high epsilon coefficients (500-1000)\n- Universal initializations: Use linear interpolation and sine waves\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Time budget: 1300s allowed\n- Time estimates:\n  * n=256: 100s\n  * Each doubling: approx 250s\n- Must break out of loops if time exhausted\n- Always project to valid solution before evaluations\n- Include forced restarts (random mutation) if no progress\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Every candidate must pass: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and sum(h)=n/2\n2. Evaluate C5 using: dx * max(correlation_vector)\n3. Return formatted (h_values, c5, n) with tuples after forced projection\n4. Include formatted print of best progress\n\n\n_Phase_sequence:\n1. Parent validation (if provided)\n2. Base initialization (n=256 to 4096)\n3. Lag identification and focus\n4. Adaptive coordinate descent:\n   For k in [-3,0,1,2,3]:\n      Step h[i] away from (0,1) by \u00b1(h[i]*(k-index)) where k is focus lag\n5. Constrained local optimization (SLSQP) with target at active lag\n6. Random neighborhood search (\u00b15% values) to escape local minima\n7. Final comparison of scores\n\nConstraints handling:\nHandle all constraints by projection if values outside [0,1] or sum\u2260n/2, after all optimization steps\n\nOutput formatting:\nEnsure integer n multiples of 16\nUse linear interpolation for resolution jumps\nImplement restarts if dC5/step < 0.00005 for 100 steps",
